      "id": 1,
      "text": "Aaron and Elvira Putnam settled near a big bend in the Fox River in 1837, giving Big Bend its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.villageofbigbend.com/residents/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The earliest known residents of Big Bend and the surrounding area were the mound builders, who left behind conical and effigy mounds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://emke.uwm.edu/entry/village-of-big-bend/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Prucius Putnam, John Dodge, and the Hazeltine brothers founded the Town of Vernon in 1836, naming it after Vermont.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://emke.uwm.edu/entry/village-of-big-bend/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Big Bend grew around the first sawmill on the Fox River's largest curve, which was opened in 1841 by Aaron and Amos Putnam.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://emke.uwm.edu/entry/village-of-big-bend/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lumber remained one of the most important industries for Big Bend well into the twentieth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://emke.uwm.edu/entry/village-of-big-bend/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Big Bend and the surrounding Town of Vernon began as farming communities before taking on a more suburban character in recent decades.",
